    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 021120\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n420 AM MST Fri Oct 2 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated 12Z Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- As high pressure builds over the region, temperatures will warm\n  back to near normal this weekend and eventually slightly above \n  normal by the middle of next week.\n\n- Periods of breezy to locally windy conditions can be expected\n  across southcentral and southwestern AZ through this weekend \n  with gusts reaching 20-30 mph at times.\n\n- Dry weather with zero rain chances will persist through this\n  weekend and into much of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nLatest GOES mid-lvl wv imagery and 500 mb streamline analysis \nreveals a ridge of high pressure building over SoCal and the Great\nBasin region while a weak area of low pressure is beginning to \ncutoff over W TX and SE NM. Dry NE flow is present over AZ which \nwill keep skies clear over the forecast area today. Ridging aloft \nis expected to be the predominant weather pattern over the Desert \nSouthwest through this weekend which will continue to to result in\ndry weather with essentially zero chances for rain anywhere in \nthe state. Due to an increased pressure gradient between the high \nto our north and low to the SE of our region, elevated easterly \nflow is expected to develop today and persist into this weekend. \nWinds will become gusty each morning and last into the early \nafternoon, reaching up to 20-30 mph at times with gusts up to 35 \nmph possible across the higher terrain of southcentral AZ. \n\nPositive hght anomalies will be most pronounced over SoCal and into \nnorthern AZ where 500 mb hghts will reach upwards of 588-590 dam \nwhich is in the 90th percentile of climatology this time of year. \nThis will result in high temperatures reaching slightly above normal \nin the upper 90s to low 100s along and west of the Colorado River \nValley through this weekend. Elsewhere across southcentral AZ, \nelevated easterly winds and higher dewpoints in the low to mid 50s \nwill keep high temperatures in a near normal range, mainly in the \nlow to mid 90s. Overnight lows will be noticeably warmer in the mid \nto upper 70s across the lower deserts over the next few nights due \nto breezy conditions developing prior to sunrise.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/... \nDeterministic and ensemble guidance shows the 500 mb ridge axis \nflattening as a weak trough passes through the Intermountain West \nearly next week before rebuilding over SoCal and the Great Basin \nregion again by mid- week. As this pattern evolves, there may be a\nbrief dip in temperatures on Monday before significant warming \noccurs from Tuesday into Thursday. Highs across the western \ndeserts will likely remain in the low 100s Monday and Tuesday \nbefore topping out around 100-105 degrees as early as Wednesday. \nThe lower deserts of southcentral AZ will also see temperatures \ngradually rise from the low to mid 90s Monday and Tuesday to \naround 100 degrees by mid-week. Overnight lows will also be warm, \ngenerally remaining in the low to mid 70s. Overall, daily \ntemperatures are expected peak around 4-8 degrees above normal mid\nto late next week. Subsidence aloft and dry air in the mid lvls \nshould keep a lid on any potential convective, even over the AZ \nhigher terrain and thus rain chances will remain near zero each \nday next week. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1120Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nNo major aviation weather concerns under clear skies can be\nexpected throughout the TAF period. Overall wind directions\nthroughout the period will be out of the east. Light speeds under\n10 kts will prevail through early this morning before gusts\nupwards of 20 kts materialize by the mid/late morning (15-16Z for\nKIWA and KPHX and around 19Z for KDVT and KSDL) through the\nafternoon hours, subsiding around sunset. Winds will remain\nelevated this evening and overnight with speeds around 7-12 kt\nexpected. Another period of gusty winds (upper teens to around 20\nkt) will then start to materialize early tomorrow morning, around\nor shortly after sunrise. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo aviation weather issues under clear skies can be expected\nthroughout the TAF period. Winds through most of this morning will\ngenerally be light and variable with periods of calm conditions\nbefore a more easterly direction materializes by the late morning\nhours with overall speeds aob 10 kts. Another period of light and\nvariable with periods of calm conditions is then expected for this\nevening and overnight into tomorrow morning. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDry conditions with near normal temperatures are anticipated through \nthis weekend with a steady warming trend expected from the beginning \nto middle of next week. MinRHs will mostly range from 15-25% over \nthe western districts to 25-35% for the eastern districts with good \nto excellent overnight recoveries. A persistent breezy easterly \ngradient wind will develop across the region today and persist for \nthe next several days. Morning to early afternoon wind gusts in the \n20-30 mph range will become common. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Salerno\nLONG TERM...Salerno \nAVIATION...Berislavich/Lojero \nFIRE WEATHER...Salerno\n",
